/* ==============================================================
   07 — Delay, the motion queue, and checkpoints
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   Delay, SetCheckpoint, and what "finished" means
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     The log panel as much as the robot
   TIME      ~20 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE MOTION QUEUE

   Every motion command you send goes into a queue inside the
   robot and is executed first in, first out. Commands are
   ACCEPTED almost instantly; they are EXECUTED in order, at the
   speed of physics.

   So there are two different clocks in play:

     - your program, racing to the end of the file
     - the robot, working through the queue behind it

   For a self-contained motion sequence this never matters. It
   matters enormously the moment the robot has to agree with
   something else about when a step is done — a vacuum cup, a
   conveyor, a camera trigger, a PLC handshake.

   DELAY: PAUSE THE QUEUE

       Delay(t)          t in seconds

   Delay is itself a motion command, so it sits in the queue and
   holds it for t seconds when it gets there. It does not pause
   your host program, and it does not pause anything else on the
   robot.

   Use it for dwells that are genuinely about time: letting an
   adhesive wet out, letting a vacuum cup build suction, letting
   a part settle before releasing it. Used in demos to make a
   sequence readable.

   Do NOT use it as a handshake. "Delay(0.5) should be enough for
   the vacuum to build" works on the bench and fails on the line
   at 3 a.m., the day a cup starts to wear. That is what
   checkpoints are for.

   SETCHECKPOINT: TELL ME WHEN YOU GET HERE

       SetCheckpoint(n)  n from 1 to 8000

   Drop this between motion commands. When the robot's execution
   actually reaches that point in the queue, it emits [3030][n].
   Your host program waits for that message and then knows — not
   estimates — that everything before it has physically
   completed.

   From the manual, and worth quoting to an integrator verbatim:
   using a checkpoint is the only reliable way to confirm that a
   specific motion sequence has completed. Do not build a
   handshake on the end-of-motion or end-of-block messages; they
   can arrive well before the sequence is finished, or not at
   all if they have not been enabled.

   One subtlety: with blending on, the checkpoint is reported
   somewhere along the blended corner, not at a dead stop. If
   the host needs the robot STOPPED at a point, turn blending
   off before it.

   If the queue is cleared, or the robot is deactivated, errors
   out, or hits a safety stop, a pending checkpoint is discarded
   and reported as [3040][n] instead — so a host waiting on it
   learns that it will never arrive, rather than hanging.

   This one command is the backbone of every real integration.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)

// --- A sequence with dwells -----------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

// Checkpoint 1: "in the start position". A host program would
// wait for [3030][1] here before releasing a part onto the
// fixture, or triggering a camera.
SetCheckpoint(1)

MovePose(180, 0, 80, 0)
SetCheckpoint(2)

// A real dwell: this is the kind of pause a process actually
// asks for — hold still while something else happens.
Delay(2)

MovePose(180, 60, 80, 0)
SetCheckpoint(3)

Delay(1)

MovePose(180, -60, 80, 0)
SetCheckpoint(4)


// --- Back to zero ---------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)

// Checkpoint 8: "cycle complete, safe to load the next part".
SetCheckpoint(8)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Open the log panel before running. Watch [3030][1] through
      [3030][8] appear as the robot physically reaches each
      point. That stream of messages IS the integration story —
      it is exactly what a PLC or a Python host subscribes to.

   2. Run the file, and while the robot is still moving, press
      the stop button. The checkpoints that had not yet been
      reached come back as [3040][n] — discarded. Show that the
      host is never left waiting on something that will not
      arrive.

   3. Ask how you currently know a robot has finished a move.
      If the answer involves a timer, you have found a real
      problem worth solving.

   NEXT   02_intermediate/01_world_reference_frame.mxprog — making
          coordinates mean something to the application.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
